- bool Jit.ArmLse, UNKNOWN
- bool Jit.ArmForceFarLiteral = false, UNKNOWN

  Relocatable ARM code always emits ADRP/LDR far literal loads and relies on
  relocation to convert them to direct LDR literal loads when possible.
  Non-relocatable ARM code emits near literal loads by default; this option
  forces it to emit far literal loads when a non-relocatable path needs the
  wider reach because relocation will not adjust the literal form later.

- bool Jit.ArmDisableAdrpLdrShortening = false, UNKNOWN

  When true, ARM relocation keeps ADRP/LDR literal-load pairs instead of
  shortening them to a direct LDR literal load when the final literal is in
  range. This is useful for stress-testing ADRP/LDR far literal support.

- bool Jit.ArmLoadElimVerify = false, rehn

  Correctness-checking mode for the ARM vasm load-elimination pass
  (eliminateRedundantLoads). When true, a redundant load is NOT replaced by a
  copy of the previously-available value; instead the original load is kept and
  a runtime check is emitted after it that traps if the loaded value differs
  from the value the pass would have forwarded. This turns any unsound alias
  analysis in the pass into an immediate crash at the offending site. Adds a
  reload, a compare and a branchless faulting store per eliminated load, so it
  is for debugging only and must stay off in production.

- bool Jit.ArmInlineSmashableCall = true, rehn

  When true, ARM smashable Call instructions use the inline pattern
  (LDR + BLR), embedding the indirect call in the instruction stream.
  When false, the veneer pattern is used (BL to a separate
  LDR + BR veneer).

- bool Jit.ArmInlineSmashableJmp = true, rehn

  When true, ARM smashable Jmp instructions use the inline pattern
  (LDR + BR), embedding the indirect jump in the instruction stream.
  When false, the veneer pattern is used (B to a separate
  LDR + BR veneer).

- bool Jit.ArmSmashLiteralOnly = false, rehn

  When true, smashing an ARM call/jmp/jcc writes only the pooled literal and
  leaves the branch indirect, instead of collapsing the site to a direct
  branch once the target is in range. Collapsing is one-way: the literal
  stops being what the branch reads, so a later re-smash of a still-tracked
  site finds nothing to patch and is silently dropped. Callers that
  legitimately re-smash -- rebinding a caller from a profiling prologue to an
  optimized one, redirecting incoming branches before a translation is
  reclaimed, installing a debugger guard -- get no effect. Staying indirect
  keeps every site re-smashable for its whole life, at the cost of one
  indirect branch per smashable site.

